WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.247
Welcome to part two, the part
with the most exam points.

2
00:00:04.297 --> 00:00:07.468
Last year, three of the ten
problems came from this

3
00:00:07.508 --> 00:00:10.742
lecture, a noise-figure
cascade, a cascade intercept

4
00:00:10.782 --> 00:00:14.079
point and a compression and
noise-floor question, and

5
00:00:14.119 --> 00:00:18.939
almost every other problem needs the
decibel conversions we start with.

6
00:00:18.989 --> 00:00:22.284
We start with decibels, then
noise and the noise figure,

7
00:00:22.324 --> 00:00:25.499
then non-linearity and
intercept points, and we finish

8
00:00:25.539 --> 00:00:29.521
with sensitivity and dynamic
range, which combine everything.

9
00:00:31.171 --> 00:00:34.118
Radio engineers work in
decibels because gains

10
00:00:34.158 --> 00:00:38.208
multiply and signal levels
span many orders of magnitude.

11
00:00:38.258 --> 00:00:42.363
A power ratio in decibels is 10
times the logarithm of the ratio,

12
00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:48.906
so doubling the power adds 3.01 dB
and multiplying by ten adds 10 dB.

13
00:00:48.956 --> 00:00:54.079
An absolute power needs a reference:
dBW is relative to one watt, and

14
00:00:54.119 --> 00:01:00.359
dBm to one milliwatt, so the dBm value
is always the dBW value plus 30.

15
00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:03.990
Voltages use 20 times the
logarithm, because power goes

16
00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:07.305
with the square of the voltage,
and the simple formula

17
00:01:07.345 --> 00:01:11.255
holds only when both voltages
are across equal impedances.

18
00:01:11.305 --> 00:01:16.411
Voltage levels are given in dBV,
relative to one volt, or dBµV,

19
00:01:16.451 --> 00:01:23.868
relative to one microvolt, and the
dBµV value is the dBV value plus 120.

20
00:01:23.918 --> 00:01:27.797
Keep the rule in mind: 10
log for powers, 20 log for

21
00:01:27.837 --> 00:01:32.175
voltages, and a plain dB is
always a ratio, never a level.

22
00:01:33.825 --> 00:01:37.008
Let us do the three
conversions from the slides.

23
00:01:37.058 --> 00:01:47.694
Ten times the logarithm of 0.05 is minus
13.01, so 0.05 watts is minus 13.01 dBW.

24
00:01:47.744 --> 00:01:52.572
Adding 30 gives 16.99 dBm,
which you can check directly:

25
00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:57.951
50 milliwatts over one milliwatt,
ten log of 50, is 16.99.

26
00:01:58.001 --> 00:02:01.483
For the voltage, 20 times
the logarithm of ten to the

27
00:02:01.523 --> 00:02:07.743
minus 3 is minus 60 dBV, and
adding 120 gives 60 dBµV.

28
00:02:07.793 --> 00:02:13.100
Going back from minus 70 dBm, divide by
ten and raise ten to that power: ten

29
00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:19.417
to the minus 7 milliwatts, which is 0.1
nanowatts, a typical received signal.

30
00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:24.969
Decibels make cascades easy,
but there is one classic trap.

31
00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:31.037
Gains in dB add, and a level in dBm plus
a gain in dB gives a level in dBm, so 30

32
00:02:31.077 --> 00:02:37.504
dBm through a 10 dB amplifier is 40 dBm;
but two levels in dBm cannot be added:

33
00:02:37.544 --> 00:02:45.246
30 dBm plus 30 dBm is one watt plus one
watt, two watts, which is 33 dBm, not 60.

34
00:02:45.296 --> 00:02:47.909
For part a, 200 milliwatts out for one

35
00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:53.204
milliwatt in is a gain of
200, which is 23.01 dB.

36
00:02:53.254 --> 00:02:56.322
For part b, the attenuator
halves the power, a loss

37
00:02:56.362 --> 00:03:02.625
of 2, which is 3.01 dB, or
a gain of minus 3.01 dB.

38
00:03:02.675 --> 00:03:07.651
For part c, convert each level
to milliwatts: one, plus 1.995,

39
00:03:07.691 --> 00:03:14.898
plus 0.251, which is 3.246 milliwatts,
and ten log of that is 5.11

40
00:03:14.938 --> 00:03:19.685
dBm: barely more than the largest
term, as always when adding powers.

41
00:03:19.735 --> 00:03:24.217
And for part d, the gains in
dB simply add, 3 times 23

42
00:03:24.257 --> 00:03:29.396
minus 3 is 66 dB, a linear
gain of almost four million.

43
00:03:31.046 --> 00:03:33.749
Noise sets the smallest
signal a receiver can

44
00:03:33.789 --> 00:03:37.248
detect, so it limits the
range of every radio link.

45
00:03:37.298 --> 00:03:41.086
The basic source is thermal
noise: in any resistor above

46
00:03:41.126 --> 00:03:44.983
absolute zero, the random
agitation of charges produces a

47
00:03:45.023 --> 00:03:49.954
noise voltage whose r m s value
is the square root of 4 k T B R.

48
00:03:50.004 --> 00:03:55.810
With Boltzmann's constant, 1.38 times
ten to the minus 23 joules per kelvin, a

49
00:03:55.850 --> 00:04:02.881
50 ohm resistor at 290 kelvin in a 1
megahertz bandwidth gives 0.89 microvolts.

50
00:04:02.931 --> 00:04:06.020
The power that this resistor
delivers to a matched load is

51
00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.702
simply k T B, which does not
depend on the resistance at all.

52
00:04:09.752 --> 00:04:15.429
At the reference temperature T zero of
290 kelvin, k T zero is 4 times ten

53
00:04:15.469 --> 00:04:21.504
to the minus 21 watts per hertz, which
is minus 204 dBW per hertz, or minus

54
00:04:21.544 --> 00:04:27.969
174 dBm per hertz: memorise this number,
it appears in every noise problem.

55
00:04:28.019 --> 00:04:31.181
Devices add other noise as
well: shot noise from the

56
00:04:31.221 --> 00:04:34.629
discrete nature of the current,
and flicker noise, which

57
00:04:34.669 --> 00:04:39.329
grows as one over the frequency and
matters mostly at low frequencies.

58
00:04:40.979 --> 00:04:44.171
The quality of the received
signal is measured by the

59
00:04:44.211 --> 00:04:49.561
signal-to-noise ratio, the signal power
over the noise power, usually in decibels.

60
00:04:49.611 --> 00:04:55.160
Every real component adds noise of its
own, so the SNR gets worse from input

61
00:04:55.200 --> 00:05:00.750
to output, and the noise figure measures
by how much: the input SNR over the

62
00:05:00.790 --> 00:05:07.016
output SNR, at least one, or in decibels
the input SNR minus the output SNR.

63
00:05:07.066 --> 00:05:09.557
It is defined with a
matched noise source at the

64
00:05:09.597 --> 00:05:13.534
reference temperature of
290 kelvin at the input.

65
00:05:13.584 --> 00:05:18.542
Equivalently, the component can be
described by its equivalent noise

66
00:05:18.582 --> 00:05:23.833
temperature T e: the output noise is
k B G times T zero plus T e, so the

67
00:05:23.873 --> 00:05:29.956
noise factor is one plus T e over T zero,
and T e is T zero times F minus one.

68
00:05:30.006 --> 00:05:35.066
A practical warning: the formulas use the
linear factor F, while datasheets give

69
00:05:35.106 --> 00:05:40.619
the noise figure in decibels, so always
convert before using the cascade formula.

70
00:05:42.269 --> 00:05:44.451
When several stages are cascaded, their

71
00:05:44.491 --> 00:05:47.519
noise combines according
to the Friis formula.

72
00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:51.436
The total noise factor is the first
factor, plus the second factor

73
00:05:51.476 --> 00:05:55.461
minus one divided by the first gain,
plus the third factor minus one

74
00:05:55.501 --> 00:06:00.469
divided by the product of the first two
gains, and so on, all in linear units.

75
00:06:00.519 --> 00:06:04.110
Each stage's own noise is divided
by the gain in front of it,

76
00:06:04.150 --> 00:06:08.369
because the signal has already
been amplified when it gets there.

77
00:06:08.419 --> 00:06:12.242
So, provided its gain is high,
the first stage dominates, and

78
00:06:12.282 --> 00:06:16.042
that is why every receiver starts
with a low-noise amplifier

79
00:06:16.082 --> 00:06:19.651
with a high gain: the stages
after it then hardly matter;

80
00:06:19.691 --> 00:06:24.094
with a lossy or low-gain first
stage, compare the terms instead.

81
00:06:24.144 --> 00:06:28.868
A passive lossy component, such as a
filter, a cable or a passive mixer at

82
00:06:28.908 --> 00:06:33.631
room temperature, has a gain of one
over its loss and a noise factor equal

83
00:06:33.671 --> 00:06:38.914
to its loss, which is why a lossy cable
in front of the LNA is so harmful.

84
00:06:38.964 --> 00:06:44.167
And the total gain in dB is just the sum
of the gains minus the sum of the losses.

85
00:06:45.817 --> 00:06:48.489
This is the worked
example from the slides.

86
00:06:48.539 --> 00:06:53.388
First convert everything to
linear: gains of 10, 0.794

87
00:06:53.428 --> 00:07:02.078
and 0.501, and noise factors
of 1.585, 1.259 and 2.512.

88
00:07:02.128 --> 00:07:08.808
Friis gives 1.585, plus 0.259
over 10, plus 1.512 over 10

89
00:07:08.848 --> 00:07:15.412
times 0.794, which is 1.80,
or 2.56 dB: notice how little

90
00:07:15.452 --> 00:07:19.393
the filter and the mixer add
after the 10 dB amplifier.

91
00:07:19.443 --> 00:07:25.633
The gain is 10 minus 1 minus 3,
which is 6 dB, a factor of 3.98.

92
00:07:25.683 --> 00:07:33.127
The equivalent noise temperature
is 290 times 0.80, 232 kelvin.

93
00:07:33.177 --> 00:07:37.396
The antenna is colder than T
zero, 150 kelvin, so the output

94
00:07:37.436 --> 00:07:43.949
noise is k B G times 150 plus
232: 1.38 times ten to the minus

95
00:07:43.989 --> 00:07:50.830
23, times ten to the 7, times
3.98, times 382, which is 2.1

96
00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:55.417
times ten to the minus 13 watts,
minus 96.8 dBm; the slide

97
00:07:55.457 --> 00:08:01.159
rounds it to 2.08 times ten to the
minus 13 watts with the same dBm value.

98
00:08:02.809 --> 00:08:07.443
This was the first problem of last
year's exam, and it is pure Friis.

99
00:08:07.493 --> 00:08:10.904
Two dB is a factor of 1.585, so the total

100
00:08:10.944 --> 00:08:17.630
factor is 1.585 plus 0.585
over 10, which is 1.643.

101
00:08:17.680 --> 00:08:24.752
In decibels that is 2.16 dB: the
second amplifier adds only 0.16 dB.

102
00:08:24.802 --> 00:08:32.056
The equivalent noise temperature
is 290 times 0.643, 186.6

103
00:08:32.096 --> 00:08:38.771
kelvin; one solved copy wrote 185.6
because it rounded F to 1.64.

104
00:08:38.821 --> 00:08:44.754
For the output noise, add the
decibels: minus 174 dBm per hertz,

105
00:08:44.794 --> 00:08:49.608
plus the noise figure, plus ten log
of the bandwidth, plus the gain.

106
00:08:49.658 --> 00:08:56.699
That is minus 174 plus 2.16
plus 64.77 plus 20, which

107
00:08:56.739 --> 00:09:01.409
is minus 87.1 dBm, exactly
as in both solved copies.

108
00:09:03.059 --> 00:09:05.685
In decibels the noise
calculations become a

109
00:09:05.725 --> 00:09:08.866
sum, which the bar chart
builds step by step.

110
00:09:08.916 --> 00:09:13.607
Start from minus 174 dBm per
hertz, add ten log of the

111
00:09:13.647 --> 00:09:16.645
bandwidth, add the noise
figure, and add the gain if

112
00:09:16.685 --> 00:09:20.423
you want the noise at the
output: that is the noise floor.

113
00:09:20.473 --> 00:09:26.651
Without the gain, the same sum is the
noise referred to the input, k T zero B F.

114
00:09:26.701 --> 00:09:30.285
The sensitivity, the weakest
signal that still gives the

115
00:09:30.325 --> 00:09:34.946
required output SNR, is the input
noise plus that minimum SNR.

116
00:09:34.996 --> 00:09:40.275
With a 1 megahertz bandwidth, a 5
dB noise figure and a 10 dB minimum

117
00:09:40.315 --> 00:09:48.001
SNR, the bars give minus 174 plus 60
plus 5 plus 10, which is minus 99 dBm.

118
00:09:48.051 --> 00:09:51.867
Notice that each decade of
bandwidth costs 10 dB, so a

119
00:09:51.907 --> 00:09:56.365
narrow-band receiver is much more
sensitive than a wide-band one.

120
00:09:58.015 --> 00:10:01.982
Every real amplifier is non-linear,
and we model its output as a

121
00:10:02.022 --> 00:10:05.864
power series of the input: alpha
zero plus alpha one times the

122
00:10:05.904 --> 00:10:10.637
input plus alpha two times its square
plus alpha three times its cube.

123
00:10:10.687 --> 00:10:13.979
With one tone at the input,
the squared and cubed terms

124
00:10:14.019 --> 00:10:17.736
generate a DC term and harmonics
at twice and three times the

125
00:10:17.776 --> 00:10:22.124
frequency; they are far from the
signal, so a filter removes them.

126
00:10:22.174 --> 00:10:24.865
The cubic term also
changes the fundamental:

127
00:10:24.905 --> 00:10:27.719
its gain becomes alpha
one plus three quarters

128
00:10:27.759 --> 00:10:30.635
alpha three A squared,
and since alpha three is

129
00:10:30.675 --> 00:10:33.242
negative in practical amplifiers, the gain

130
00:10:33.282 --> 00:10:37.107
falls as the amplitude grows,
which is gain compression.

131
00:10:37.157 --> 00:10:40.866
The real problem appears with
two close tones: the cubic term

132
00:10:40.906 --> 00:10:44.555
produces third-order intermodulation
products at twice omega

133
00:10:44.595 --> 00:10:48.366
one minus omega two and twice
omega two minus omega one, right

134
00:10:48.406 --> 00:10:52.936
next to the wanted signals, in red,
where no filter can remove them.

135
00:10:54.586 --> 00:10:58.609
On a plot of output power against
input power, both in dBm, the

136
00:10:58.649 --> 00:11:02.485
fundamental is a straight line
of slope one until it starts to

137
00:11:02.525 --> 00:11:08.938
compress; the 1 dB compression point is
where it falls 1 dB below the ideal line.

138
00:11:08.988 --> 00:11:13.703
The third-order products grow with the
cube of the input, so on the decibel plot

139
00:11:13.743 --> 00:11:19.798
their line has a slope of 3: every extra
dB of input adds 3 dB of intermodulation.

140
00:11:19.848 --> 00:11:24.048
Extending both straight lines, they
meet at the third-order intercept

141
00:11:24.088 --> 00:11:30.520
point; its input coordinate is the IIP3,
and its output coordinate the OIP3.

142
00:11:30.570 --> 00:11:36.562
The two are related by the gain, OIP3
equals IIP3 plus G, and for a cubic

143
00:11:36.602 --> 00:11:43.865
non-linearity the 1 dB compression
point lies about 9.6 dB below the IIP3.

144
00:11:43.915 --> 00:11:47.248
A useful extra: the
input-referred intermodulation

145
00:11:47.288 --> 00:11:50.757
level is 3 times the input
power minus twice the

146
00:11:50.797 --> 00:11:54.738
IIP3, so the
carrier-to-intermodulation ratio is

147
00:11:54.778 --> 00:11:59.648
twice the distance between
the IIP3 and the input power.

148
00:12:01.298 --> 00:12:05.117
For linearity the cascade formula
works the other way round from

149
00:12:05.157 --> 00:12:09.337
Friis: the reciprocal of the
total IIP3 is the sum of each

150
00:12:09.377 --> 00:12:13.799
stage's reciprocal IIP3, multiplied
by the gain in front of it,

151
00:12:13.839 --> 00:12:17.658
so a stage weighs more the more
gain sits in front of it and the

152
00:12:17.698 --> 00:12:23.173
lower its own IIP3: later stages are
often the bottleneck, but not always.

153
00:12:23.223 --> 00:12:28.216
Convert to linear: gains
of 1.585, 0.316 and

154
00:12:28.256 --> 00:12:33.883
100, and intercepts of 10,
39.8 and 100 milliwatts.

155
00:12:33.933 --> 00:12:40.277
The sum is one over 10, plus
1.585 over 39.8, plus 1.585

156
00:12:40.317 --> 00:12:46.221
times 0.316 over 100, which
is 0.1448 per milliwatt; here

157
00:12:46.261 --> 00:12:51.009
the first stage contributes
69 %, the second 27 % and the

158
00:12:51.049 --> 00:12:55.027
last only 3.5 %, because the
attenuator in front of it

159
00:12:55.067 --> 00:12:58.884
cuts its gain, so compare the
terms instead of trusting a rule.

160
00:12:58.934 --> 00:13:03.441
Its inverse is 6.91 milliwatts, 8.4 dBm.

161
00:13:03.491 --> 00:13:12.026
The total gain is 2 minus 5 plus
20, 17 dB, so the OIP3 is 25.4 dBm.

162
00:13:12.076 --> 00:13:15.975
And the 1 dB compression
point is about 9.6

163
00:13:16.015 --> 00:13:20.750
dB below the IIP3, minus
1.2 dBm at the input.

164
00:13:22.400 --> 00:13:26.523
This budget problem combines
all three cascade calculations.

165
00:13:26.573 --> 00:13:33.866
The gain is the easy part: minus 2, plus
10, minus 4, plus 20, which is 24 dB.

166
00:13:33.916 --> 00:13:38.678
For the noise figure, the figure gives
no noise figure for the filter and the

167
00:13:38.718 --> 00:13:45.179
mixer, so take each passive part's noise
figure equal to its loss: 2 dB and 4 dB.

168
00:13:45.229 --> 00:13:52.972
Friis then gives 1.585 plus
0.927 plus 0.240 plus 0.396,

169
00:13:53.012 --> 00:14:00.115
which is 3.148, or 4.98 dB; the
filter in front costs a full 2 dB.

170
00:14:00.165 --> 00:14:04.897
For the intercept point, the
figure just says IP3; in Pozar,

171
00:14:04.937 --> 00:14:09.453
where this figure comes from, it
means the output intercept, so

172
00:14:09.493 --> 00:14:14.949
subtract each stage's gain: the
RF amplifier has an IIP3 of 0

173
00:14:14.989 --> 00:14:23.004
dBm, the mixer 15 plus 4, 19 dBm, and
the IF amplifier 20 minus 20, 0 dBm.

174
00:14:23.054 --> 00:14:30.557
The cascade sum is 0.631, plus
6.31 over 79.4, plus 2.51,

175
00:14:30.597 --> 00:14:36.458
which is 3.22 per milliwatt,
so the input IP3 is minus 5.08

176
00:14:36.498 --> 00:14:41.167
dBm: the IF amplifier, with the full
gain in front of it, dominates.

177
00:14:41.217 --> 00:14:45.830
If you read the IP3 values as input
intercepts instead, the answer is

178
00:14:45.870 --> 00:14:51.679
plus 5.41 dBm, so on the exam always
write down which reading you used.

179
00:14:53.329 --> 00:14:57.964
Last year's exam had a cascade intercept
question: one solved copy crossed

180
00:14:58.004 --> 00:15:02.828
it out, and the other, where it is
problem 1, got it wrong, so let us do it

181
00:15:02.868 --> 00:15:08.145
properly; the third block's gain is hidden
on the copy, but it hardly matters.

182
00:15:08.195 --> 00:15:11.922
The datasheet values are
output intercepts, so first

183
00:15:11.962 --> 00:15:16.775
subtract each stage's gain:
the LNA has an IIP3 of 10

184
00:15:16.815 --> 00:15:23.394
minus 20, minus 10 dBm, and the
mixer 5 minus 15, also minus 10 dBm.

185
00:15:23.444 --> 00:15:29.060
In milliwatts, the sum is one over
0.1, plus 100 over 0.1, plus a

186
00:15:29.100 --> 00:15:34.510
negligible term for the very linear
filter, about 1 010 per milliwatt.

187
00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:39.742
So the system IIP3 is about one
microwatt, minus 30 dBm, set almost

188
00:15:39.782 --> 00:15:44.840
entirely by the mixer, which sees
the full 20 dB of LNA gain; assuming

189
00:15:44.880 --> 00:15:54.369
the third block is a 2.5 dB loss, the net
gain is 32.5 dB and the OIP3 is 2.5 dBm.

190
00:15:54.419 --> 00:15:59.391
With the given input of minus 40 dBm,
the intermodulation products sit twice

191
00:15:59.431 --> 00:16:04.650
10, that is 20 dB, below the carriers,
and since all the noise figures are

192
00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:12.218
given, Friis gives a total noise factor
of 1.615, a noise figure of 2.08 dB.

193
00:16:12.268 --> 00:16:15.748
The classmate's copy put the
output intercepts straight

194
00:16:15.788 --> 00:16:19.908
into the formula as input
intercepts and got minus 15 dBm,

195
00:16:19.948 --> 00:16:25.738
which was marked incorrect: always
convert OIP3 to IIP3 first.

196
00:16:27.388 --> 00:16:30.778
The sensitivity is the minimum
input signal for which

197
00:16:30.818 --> 00:16:34.727
the receiver still delivers
an acceptable output SNR.

198
00:16:34.777 --> 00:16:38.861
Start from the definition of the
noise factor with the output SNR

199
00:16:38.901 --> 00:16:43.498
at its minimum allowed value and the
input noise equal to k T zero B.

200
00:16:43.548 --> 00:16:47.727
Solving for the signal, the
minimum power is k T zero, times

201
00:16:47.767 --> 00:16:52.547
the bandwidth, times the noise
factor, times the minimum SNR.

202
00:16:52.597 --> 00:16:58.027
In decibels, the sensitivity
in dBm is minus 174, plus the

203
00:16:58.067 --> 00:17:02.792
noise figure, plus ten log of the
bandwidth, plus the minimum SNR.

204
00:17:02.842 --> 00:17:06.093
For a digital link the
minimum SNR comes from a

205
00:17:06.133 --> 00:17:10.646
bit-error-rate curve: the SNR
that gives the required BER.

206
00:17:10.696 --> 00:17:14.750
And the formula tells you how
to improve a receiver: a better

207
00:17:14.790 --> 00:17:19.637
LNA, a narrower bandwidth, or a
modulation that needs less SNR.

208
00:17:21.287 --> 00:17:25.341
The satellite problem runs the
sensitivity formula backwards.

209
00:17:25.391 --> 00:17:30.993
First convert the BER: 0.001 %
is ten to the minus 5, and on

210
00:17:31.033 --> 00:17:36.399
the slide's curve that level is
reached at an SNR of about 17 dB.

211
00:17:36.449 --> 00:17:40.338
Then solve the sensitivity
formula for the noise figure: the

212
00:17:40.378 --> 00:17:47.538
sensitivity, plus 174, minus ten log of
the bandwidth, minus the minimum SNR.

213
00:17:47.588 --> 00:17:51.973
Ten log of 3 000 is 34.77,
so the noise figure is

214
00:17:52.013 --> 00:18:00.349
minus 118 plus 174 minus
34.77 minus 17, about 4.2 dB.

215
00:18:00.399 --> 00:18:04.008
Because the SNR is read from
a graph, a dB either way is

216
00:18:04.048 --> 00:18:08.551
acceptable, so write down the value
you read and carry it through.

217
00:18:10.201 --> 00:18:13.230
The dynamic range is the
ratio between the largest and

218
00:18:13.270 --> 00:18:17.029
the smallest levels for which
the system behaves as desired.

219
00:18:17.079 --> 00:18:21.967
The bottom is set by the noise
floor, minus 174 plus the noise

220
00:18:22.007 --> 00:18:25.422
figure plus ten log of the
bandwidth plus the gain, typically

221
00:18:25.462 --> 00:18:30.003
between minus 60 and minus 100
dBm over the system bandwidth.

222
00:18:30.053 --> 00:18:33.895
The top is set by compression, so
the linear dynamic range at the

223
00:18:33.935 --> 00:18:39.122
output is the output 1 dB compression
point minus the output noise floor.

224
00:18:39.172 --> 00:18:41.834
Referred to the input, it is the input 1

225
00:18:41.874 --> 00:18:44.990
dB compression point
minus the sensitivity.

226
00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:48.457
To move the compression point
between input and output,

227
00:18:48.497 --> 00:18:52.040
remember that the gain there
is already compressed by one

228
00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:56.641
dB, so the input point is the output
point minus the gain plus one.

229
00:18:58.291 --> 00:19:00.974
Two quick output dynamic ranges.

230
00:19:01.024 --> 00:19:06.252
In the example, the noise floor
is minus 174 plus 7 plus ten

231
00:19:06.292 --> 00:19:11.672
log of ten to the 8, which is 80,
plus 40, giving minus 47 dBm.

232
00:19:11.722 --> 00:19:17.316
The dynamic range is 25 minus minus
47, which is 72 dB; the slide

233
00:19:17.356 --> 00:19:22.374
also does it in linear units, 316
milliwatts over 2 times ten to

234
00:19:22.414 --> 00:19:28.628
the minus 5 milliwatts, 1.58 times
ten to the 7, the same 72 dB.

235
00:19:28.678 --> 00:19:32.777
In the problem, the
noise floor is minus 174

236
00:19:32.817 --> 00:19:39.105
plus 6 plus 73.01 plus 30,
which is minus 65.0 dBm.

237
00:19:39.155 --> 00:19:44.393
So the output dynamic range
is 21 plus 65, 86 dB.

238
00:19:46.043 --> 00:19:49.679
The last problem on the slides
puts everything together.

239
00:19:49.729 --> 00:19:55.635
The gain is minus 1 plus 10 minus 4
plus 20, 25 dB, and Friis, with the

240
00:19:55.675 --> 00:20:03.012
passive filter and mixer at their losses,
gives a noise factor of 2.66, 4.25 dB.

241
00:20:03.062 --> 00:20:07.194
The IF bandwidth is 500 megahertz,
so the sensitivity is minus

242
00:20:07.234 --> 00:20:15.754
174 plus 4.25 plus 87 plus the
required 20 dB, minus 62.8 dBm.

243
00:20:15.804 --> 00:20:21.249
Reading the IP3 values as output
intercepts, as in the previous budget

244
00:20:21.289 --> 00:20:27.528
problem, the input intercepts are 0
dBm for the RF amplifier, 13 plus 4,

245
00:20:27.568 --> 00:20:34.846
17 dBm for the mixer, and 16 minus
20, minus 4 dBm for the IF amplifier.

246
00:20:34.896 --> 00:20:41.928
The cascade sum is 0.794 plus
7.94 over 50.1 plus 3.16 over

247
00:20:41.968 --> 00:20:49.793
0.398, which is 8.89 per milliwatt,
so the input IP3 is minus 9.5 dBm.

248
00:20:49.843 --> 00:20:57.403
The 1 dB compression point is about
9.6 dB lower, minus 19.1 dBm.

249
00:20:57.453 --> 00:21:05.082
And the input dynamic range is minus
19.1 minus minus 62.8, which is 43.7 dB.

250
00:21:06.732 --> 00:21:09.100
The last problem of last year's exam was

251
00:21:09.140 --> 00:21:12.319
given as a graph of output
against input power.

252
00:21:12.369 --> 00:21:17.780
At the 1 dB compression point the
gain is 12 dB instead of 13, so

253
00:21:17.820 --> 00:21:22.742
the input point is 20 minus 12,
8 dBm; one solved copy wrote 7,

254
00:21:22.782 --> 00:21:27.359
forgetting that the gain is
compressed, so the exact answer is 8.

255
00:21:27.409 --> 00:21:38.347
Then the IIP3 is about 9.6 dB higher, 17.6
dBm, and the OIP3 adds the gain, 30.6 dBm.

256
00:21:38.397 --> 00:21:43.806
For the noise floor, the bandwidth from
1 to 10 GHz is 9 GHz, ten log of which

257
00:21:43.846 --> 00:21:55.094
is 99.54, so the floor is minus 174 plus
3.5 plus 99.54 plus 13, minus 57.96 dBm.

258
00:21:55.144 --> 00:21:59.003
That gives an output
dynamic range of 78 dB, a

259
00:21:59.043 --> 00:22:02.239
useful extra line if the
question asks for it.

260
00:22:03.889 --> 00:22:07.838
Before the summary, the traps
that cost points last year.

261
00:22:07.888 --> 00:22:13.637
Never add levels in dBm: convert to
milliwatts, add, and convert back.

262
00:22:13.687 --> 00:22:16.451
Both cascade formulas need linear values,

263
00:22:16.491 --> 00:22:20.260
noise factors, gains and
intercepts in milliwatts.

264
00:22:20.310 --> 00:22:24.699
In the Friis formula the first stage
dominates if its gain is high, while in

265
00:22:24.739 --> 00:22:29.761
the intercept cascade each stage weighs
its preceding gain over its own IIP3,

266
00:22:29.801 --> 00:22:34.522
which often makes a late stage the
bottleneck, but always compare the terms.

267
00:22:34.572 --> 00:22:37.153
A passive lossy stage has a gain of minus

268
00:22:37.193 --> 00:22:40.483
its loss and a noise
figure equal to its loss.

269
00:22:40.533 --> 00:22:44.081
If a datasheet gives the output
intercept, subtract the gain

270
00:22:44.121 --> 00:22:48.058
to get the input intercept before
using the cascade formula.

271
00:22:48.108 --> 00:22:51.828
And remember that the input
compression point is the output one

272
00:22:51.868 --> 00:22:57.200
minus the gain plus one, and that
minus 174 dBm per hertz holds

273
00:22:57.240 --> 00:23:02.901
only at 290 kelvin; for another source
temperature use k T B directly.

274
00:23:04.551 --> 00:23:09.639
To summarise part two: convert
between watts, dBW and dBm, between

275
00:23:09.679 --> 00:23:16.476
volts, dBV and dBµV, and use 10 log
for powers and 20 log for voltages.

276
00:23:16.526 --> 00:23:21.274
Noise starts at minus 174 dBm
per hertz, the equivalent

277
00:23:21.314 --> 00:23:24.369
temperature is T zero times
F minus one, and cascades

278
00:23:24.409 --> 00:23:28.788
follow Friis, with the first stage
dominating when its gain is high.

279
00:23:28.838 --> 00:23:32.082
Linearity is described by the
compression point and the

280
00:23:32.122 --> 00:23:36.739
intercept point, with OIP3
equal to IIP3 plus the gain,

281
00:23:36.779 --> 00:23:41.873
the compression point about
9.6 dB below the IIP3, and the

282
00:23:41.913 --> 00:23:45.965
intercept cascade weighted by
the gain in front of each stage.

283
00:23:46.015 --> 00:23:49.149
The sensitivity and the noise
floor are the same decibel

284
00:23:49.189 --> 00:23:52.607
sum with the minimum SNR or the
gain added, and the dynamic

285
00:23:52.647 --> 00:23:56.512
range is the distance from the
floor to the compression point.

286
00:23:56.562 --> 00:23:58.680
In part three we move to transmission

287
00:23:58.720 --> 00:24:01.869
lines, reflection, the
Smith chart and matching.
